course: dictionary
lesson: 6993
title: Stories with a hidden meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A parable is a short story that teaches a lesson. The best known parables were told by Jesus in the Bible, but any simple story that teaches a moral or a religious truth can be called a parable. Parables use everyday things like seeds, coins, sheep and bread, so the listener can picture them easily.
explain: The story is not only about the thing it describes. «In the Parable of the Sower seed fell on the path, on rocky ground, among thorns and on good soil.» The seed is a clue: the story is really about how different people listen and respond. Ask yourself: what does the everyday thing stand for?
explain: Parables make you think. «A parable is a short story Jesus told about everyday life with a deeper meaning about God and how to live; it makes the listener think and see himself in it.» Many people find parables puzzling, so readers talk about what they mean and do not always agree.
fact: A parable is a short story with a lesson, often about how to live or about God.
fact: Famous parables include the Good Samaritan, the Prodigal Son, the Lost Sheep and the Sower.
fact: Parables use everyday things such as seeds, coins, sheep and bread.
fact: In the Parable of the Talents, «a talent was a large sum of money».
fact: In RE, pupils look for the hidden meaning: the Lost Sheep shows that God cares about every single person.
exercise: What is a parable? | A set of rules for a football game | *A short story with a lesson, such as the Lost Sheep | A long list of Bible names | A poem about the weather
exercise: Which of these everyday things is used in a parable? | *Seeds | A smartphone | A video game console | A spaceship
exercise: «In the Parable of the Talents a talent was a large sum of money.» What does talent mean in this parable? | A kind of tree | A special skill, like singing well | *A large sum of money | A cup for drinking
test: Which of these is a parable? | The Gunpowder Plot | *The Prodigal Son | The Great Fire of London | The Battle of Hastings
test: «The guests all alike began to make excuses.» What does this parable warn us about? | Guests should always bring a gift | Animals can talk | *Excuses can stop us fixing the real problem | Dinner parties are always late
exam: Why do people say a parable has a hidden meaning? | It is written in a foreign language | *The story stands for a deeper lesson about God or how to live | It is a secret code that nobody can read | The story is always about sheep
